# SECTION 05 — ARCHITECTURAL CONTEXT

## Architectural Context

The COREI Workbench is the institutional runtime of the Operator Workstation.

It provides the architectural foundation that coordinates every operational capability while maintaining one coherent institutional operating environment.

Unlike conventional frontend architectures, the Workbench does not own business logic.

Business intelligence remains inside the backend platform.

The Workbench owns operational orchestration.

Its responsibilities include:

* composing operational environments,
* managing workspaces,
* hosting capabilities,
* coordinating environmental services,
* maintaining operator context,
* orchestrating institutional workflows,
* preserving operational continuity.

The Workbench therefore separates:

Institutional Intelligence

↓

Operational Experience

↓

Technology Runtime

↓

Visual Composition

Each layer remains independently evolvable.

---

## Workbench Architectural Layers

The Workbench shall be organised into four architectural layers.

### Layer 1 — Foundation Services

Institution-wide runtime services.

Examples include:

* Session Management
* Context Engine
* State Engine
* Permission Engine
* Theme Engine
* Identity Engine
* Configuration Engine
* Audit Engine

These services exist once.

Every capability consumes them.

---

### Layer 2 — Environmental Services

Services controlling the operational environment.

Examples include:

* Workspace Engine
* Layout Engine
* Window Manager
* Docking Manager
* Command Engine
* Search Engine
* Notification Engine
* History Engine
* Navigation Engine

These services define the behaviour of the Workbench itself.

---

### Layer 3 — Capability Runtime

The execution environment for institutional capabilities.

Examples include:

* Trading Runtime
* Infrastructure Runtime
* Research Runtime
* Risk Runtime
* AI Runtime
* Documentation Runtime
* Collaboration Runtime
* Executive Runtime

Capabilities operate here.

They never directly manipulate the Workbench.

---

### Layer 4 — Presentation Layer

The visible institutional environment.

Examples include:

* Workspaces
* Operational Surfaces
* Panels
* Views
* Editors
* Dashboards
* Visualisations
* Command Interfaces

Presentation becomes the final layer.

Not the architectural foundation.

---

## Workbench Responsibilities

The Workbench shall coordinate:

* capability lifecycle,
* workspace lifecycle,
* layout lifecycle,
* operator lifecycle,
* session lifecycle,
* extension lifecycle,
* environmental state,
* command routing,
* contextual awareness.

Everything else belongs elsewhere.

