### Architectural Observation

I think we've now reached what may become the COREI Runtime Law.

Instead of thinking in terms of applications, COREI should think in terms of institutional runtime.

Institution
        │
        ▼
COREI Workbench
        │
        ▼
Workbench Runtime
        │
        ├──────────────┐
        │              │
        ▼              ▼
Workbench Services   Extension Runtime
        │              │
        └──────┬───────┘
               ▼
      Operational Capabilities
               │
               ▼
     Capability Components
               │
               ▼
          Technologies

Notice the inversion.

In most frontend systems:

Components are the centre.

In COREI:

Runtime is the centre.

Everything else orbits around it.

I also think we should introduce one additional engineering principle that will influence every repository we build:

Capabilities never communicate directly with each other.

Instead, they communicate through the Workbench Runtime using well-defined services, events, commands, and contracts.

That gives COREI several long-term advantages:

Independent capability development.
Safe capability upgrades.
Deterministic lifecycle management.
Better observability.
Easier testing.
Simpler replacement of technologies.
Strong institutional governance.

This is very similar to how operating systems manage applications: applications don't own the OS—they execute within it. Likewise, COREI capabilities shouldn't own the Workbench—they should execute within it. I believe this is one of the defining architectural characteristics that will distinguish COREI OS from a conventional React application.

